A||||11||Black88GT||'88 Shocks, problemo part 2||||11||
Z||000000||Black88GT||05-17-2001||03:38 PM||||This is just great.  I finally get done the sway bar bushings and the shocks.  I go to jack the car down, and I notice I just gained about 4 inches of wheel gap.  WTF is this.  Was I supposed to compress the shocks?<P>Boy do I feel stupid (and my fiero looks pretty stupid too)<P>HELP!<P>------------------<BR>Jeff (AOL IM - FieroTT)<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif">     <BR><I>"Once you go black, you never go back..."</I><BR><I>"The Customer Can Have Any Color He Wants So Long As It's Black" </I> -Henry Ford||||reg||11
Z||000001||DaveL||05-17-2001||03:40 PM||||Did you use gas charged shocks? I can't think of any reason why changing those parts would cause the front end to rise.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||05-17-2001||03:42 PM||||Yeah they are KYB's.  I don't understand either.  I think it might have something to do with the sway bar bushings.<P>Let me go out and check.  I might not have aligned them right which would hold the upper control arm and keep the car up a little.  Hmmm let me go see<p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 05-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eric||05-17-2001||04:07 PM||||The KYBs are a little harder to compress, but if they're installed correctly, the car will be at the correct ride height after you drive it around.  Hopefully you didn't torque any bolts when the suspension was not at ride height either.  That could cause it to jack up too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||05-17-2001||04:25 PM||||Eric, thats what I did!  I checked the sway bar things and they weren't the problem.  Also in the book it says "specified torque" for the nut on the top of the shock, I didn't think it had to be any specific torque, do you know what it is?  Guess I have to go get a torque wrench.<P>Now that I screwed it up how can I figure out the proper ride height?<P>TIA||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bob Englert||05-17-2001||04:42 PM||||Jeff:<P>Noramlly the ride height would be afected by torquing the control arm bolts without putting weight on the control arm (particularly with rubber control arm bushings).  I wouldn't expect the ride height to premanently change with shocks or sway bars.<P>Have you driven the car around for a few miles to see if things settle in?<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000006||Black88GT||05-17-2001||04:44 PM||||No I haven't driven it around.  I did bounce on it a few times while praying it would go down (hehe).  You think I should drive it and see what happens?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||05-17-2001||05:57 PM||||This is common.......just drive it a bit (over speed bumps and through dips, etc) and it will settle down.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||05-17-2001||06:32 PM||||Do you have polyurethane bushings or rubber?<P>If rubber, jack the car up and loosen the bushing bolts upper and lower, support the front end up on ramps and retighten the bolts.<P>If urethane, I don't know.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Black88GT||05-17-2001||07:47 PM||||Whew.  OK I think its alright.  Its just that this is the first time I ever did the shocks/bushings so I wasn't sure.<P><BR>thanks||||reg||1
